<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mafra National Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1717">1717</a>–<a href="/years/1755">1755</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Palace of Mafra, Palace-Convent of Mafra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mafra National Palace is a huge baroque palace, basilica and Franciscan friary at Mafra, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> V from 1717 and paid for with Brazilian gold. Designed by João Frederico Ludovice, it is one of the largest palaces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and includes a famous library and two carillons. It was listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2019.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mafra-national-palace.jpg" alt="Mafra National Palace" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pedro S Bello · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Europa_-_Portugal_-_Lisboa_-_Mafra_-_Palacio_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
